"Greetings, foreigner," the priest began, "what is your name and the nature of your visit to our city?"
"I travel seeking for a chance to earn gold," answered Ragnar, looking over the skinny person, not very tall, with a clean-shaved head and chin.
"What a coincidence, I'm just looking for a man who could help me. By the way, my name is Gorro." He slightly bowed his head as a sign of respect. "I see you are a great warrior, strong and brave," said the priest with a portion of pathos, "only such a man can deal with the beast."
Listening to the priest, Ragnar was calm, even indifferent, but he livened up when he heard about the "beast."
"Do I have to kill or catch it?"
"Hmm… It would be nice if you killed the monster. First of all, it catches and eats people, and in addition to that, the beast has a bagatelle, a necklace that belongs to me. Return it to me and I will lavishly reward you."
"If I got it right, priest, that the most important is to bring the adornment back. So even if the beast is still alive, I will get my reward?" asked Ragnar without beating around the bush.
"It would be better to get the whole job done," the servant of Hanuman hesitated with some confusion, "but in general, you understood my request correctly."
"Fine," said the Lemurian without a second thought, "where can I find this beast?"
"It lives in some caves, not far from the water carriers' well. When can you go there?"
"Tomorrow morning," resolutely answered the warrior.